  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 338 PM EDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nSeasonably impressive troughing and associated surface cyclone will \nchurn east of the Great Lakes through Tuesday as ridging slides \noverhead. Subsidence provided aloft between these features will \ncontinue to build surface high pressure across the region through \nthe beginning of the week. Potent shortwave troughing progresses \nover the Great Lakes again late Tuesday night through Wednesday \nnight, bringing another system across us through the middle of the \nweek. High pressure then looks to build back in by the end of the \nweek and into this weekend.\n\nForecast Details:\n\nCloud cover across much of northern Michigan has diminished some \nover the last few hours as increasingly dry air is advected into the \narea from northwest winds and diurnal mixing has ensued. Clearing is \nexpected to continue this evening into tonight as aforementioned \nhigh pressure centers overhead, combining with weak/calm winds to \ncreate efficient radiational cooling overnight -- and likely lead to \npatchy fog early Tuesday morning. Some chilly low temperatures are \nalso anticipated across interior low-lying areas with readings \npotentially dipping well into the 40s. Dry weather will continue \nthrough Tuesday as more summer-like weather returns with partly to \nmostly sunny skies, less wind, and highs in the upper 70s and low \n80s.\n\nOur next round of shower and thunderstorm chances returns early \nWednesday morning as troughing punches across the Great Lakes. While \nsome activity will be possible early Wednesday morning ahead of the \ncold front, steadiest rain/best coverage of showers and storms is \nanticipated in the late Wednesday morning/Wednesday afternoon \ntimeframe as the front moves through. While an overall unimpressive \nenvironment looks to be in place on Wednesday given relatively \nmeager buoyancy with skinny CAPE profiles and weak shear, a few \nstrong storms cannot be completely ruled out along/ahead of the \nadvancing front on Wednesday.\n\nShower/storm chances should begin to taper Wednesday night into \nThursday, but at least slight chances for showers look to continue \non Thursday with northwest winds and lingering low-level moisture on \nthe backside of the departing system potentially support additional \nshowers across the area. Temperatures look to cool a few degrees \nduring the second half of the week after the frontal passage, \nwarming back up into the upper 70s and low 80s for the weekend.",
